From e11a25e13387eee5df54ce8f610ba1d50fff7b01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 15:04:15 GMT
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] drm/msm: Signal completed hw fences from the irq handler

Since commit 3c7a52217a8c ("drm/msm: Drop update_fences()") the irq
handler only advances the completed fence.  A submit's hw_fence, and
with it the scheduler fence that is attached to the BOs, is signalled
only when retire_submits() calls dma_fence_is_signaled() on it.
retire_submits() handles one submit at a time, and before it gets to
the next one, msm_submit_retire() takes the reservation lock of each BO
of the current submit.

So completing a fence requires dma_resv locks.  The dma-fence
cross-driver contract does not allow that: drivers may call
dma_fence_wait() while holding a dma_resv lock, so any code required
for fence completion cannot acquire one.  The msm shrinker is such a
waiter.  drm_gem_lru_scan() trylocks each BO, and active_evict() waits
for the fences of the BO with its lock held.  If an older submit that
is not retired yet uses the same BO, retire blocks on that lock, the
fences of newer, completed submits stay unsignalled, and the wait can
only end by its 10 jiffy timeout.  The scan then moves on to the next
BO and does the same.  With a mostly busy LRU a single reclaim pass
takes minutes, while the reclaiming task keeps every lock it held on
entry.

On SM8750 (Adreno 830) under memory pressure, gnome-shell entered
reclaim from the submit ioctl while holding the locks of its BOs, and
the retire worker blocked on one of them:

  [ 3142.833462] INFO: task gpu-worker:584 blocked for more than 120 seconds.
  [ 3142.833489] Call trace:
